Exactly. The real asset isn’t “we noticed Pokémon getting hot.” It’s the validated pattern extracted from what happened next.

That gives Pantheon a clean research loop:

\boxed{
\text{Notice}
\rightarrow
\text{Describe}
\rightarrow
\text{Predict}
\rightarrow
\text{Track}
\rightarrow
\text{Score}
\rightarrow
\text{Extract invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{Transfer}
\rightarrow
\text{Test again}
}

For Pokémon, the candidate pattern might be:

\text{collab frequency}\uparrow
+
\text{partner diversity}\uparrow
+
\text{category distance}\uparrow
+
\text{physical shelf commitment}\uparrow

followed by some combination of:

\text{search interest}\uparrow,\quad
\text{sell-through}\uparrow,\quad
\text{licensing income}\uparrow,\quad
\text{engagement}\uparrow,\quad
\text{related equities}\uparrow

But we don’t declare that relationship true yet. We timestamp the hypothesis and watch what actually follows.

Then the PatternDex entry becomes something like:

Cultural Surface-Area Expansion — an IP begins appearing through increasingly unrelated commercial partners and product categories, indicating that its utility to other businesses may be expanding beyond its original product market.

Now we can go hunting for the same structure elsewhere.

Disney.

Hello Kitty.

LEGO.

Formula 1.

Minecraft.

A sports league.

A musician.

An AI brand.

Even a technology standard.

The surface objects differ, but we’d search for:

\text{same expansion topology}

rather than the word “Pokémon.”

And crucially, we keep the failures too. Maybe another franchise produces fifty collaborations but consumers don’t care and nothing compounds. That teaches us that collaboration count alone is weak, while perhaps:

\text{partner diversity}
\times
\text{repeat purchases}
\times
\text{shelf persistence}

is the actual useful signal.

So every failed transfer sharpens the invariant.

I’d give every reusable pattern a Pattern Card with:

* Surface observation: what originally caught our attention.
* Candidate invariant: what we think is actually happening.
* Leading indicators: signals that appear early.
* Expected consequences: what should happen if we’re right.
* Counterexamples: situations that look similar but fail.
* Time horizon: how long the effect normally takes.
* Score: how often it has worked out-of-sample.
* Domains tested: markets, culture, biology, software, etc.
* Transfer conditions: what must remain true for the analogy to hold.

That makes PatternDex evolve from a dictionary into an empirically ranked library of transferable operators.

And eventually we can ask the Atlas something much more powerful than:

“What looks like Pokémon?”

We ask:

“Where else is Cultural Surface-Area Expansion beginning right now, before everyone recognizes it?”

That’s where the economic value appears.

Because then we’re no longer following the trend.

We’re learning the shape that tends to precede the trend.